WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.152
Idea. Look at a phone. It may be designed in one country, built in another, from parts made in many more, then sold all over the world.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:19.776
Globalisation is the world's economies becoming more connected and dependent on one another, through trade, investment, and firms working across borders.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:39.056
Different groups gain and lose. Consumers get choice and lower prices. Producers find new markets. Workers in export industries gain jobs. But traditional industries can close, pollution can rise, and a country relying on one export suffers if world prices fall.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:51.519
Why do prices fall? Foreign rivals add competition, and firms selling to the world produce on a huge scale, so cost per unit falls. Supply increases, and the price falls.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:01:06.323
Method. Step one, underline the group in the question. Step two, name a specific effect on that group. Step three, explain how globalisation causes it, step by step. Step four, use the case.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:17.823
For a six-mark Analyse, build two or three chains on the named group only. For a twelve-mark Evaluate, add real counter-points, then say what the outcome depends on.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:38.979
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2024. Global trade reached a record twenty-nine trillion dollars in 2022, and is still growing, though more slowly since the global health crisis. Analyse the possible benefits for consumers of globalisation. Six marks.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:52.701
Chain one, choice. With world trade at a record twenty-nine trillion dollars, shops can stock goods not made at home, such as tropical fruit or foreign cars. Consumers find products that suit them better.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:07.634
Chain two, prices. Home firms face foreign competition, and world-scale firms gain economies of scale. Costs fall, and some of this is passed on as lower prices, for items like clothes and electronics.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:27.236
Chain three, living standards. Lower prices mean each dollar buys more, so consumers can afford more and their standard of living rises. Chain four: technology spreads, so new products arrive sooner. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:37.275
Common mistake. A common mistake is writing about the wrong group, such as new markets for firms, when the question asks about consumers.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:43.110
Another is listing benefits in single sentences without developing any of them.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:59.715
Your turn. Now you try. Most of Country Y's exports are cocoa beans, sold to large foreign buyers. Analyse one benefit and one cost of globalisation for cocoa farmers in Country Y. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:09.003
Answer. Benefit: farmers can sell to the whole world market. More buyers mean more sales, so farm incomes rise.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:21.463
Cost: relying on one export is risky. If the world cocoa price falls, say by thirty per cent, farm incomes fall with it. And large buyers may keep most of the value.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:29.391
Remember: underline the group, name an effect, build the chain, and use the case. Now try the practice questions.
